WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.050
So I am absolutely the kind of person

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:09.000
who would take 15 minutes

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.680
trying to decide what
ice cream flavor I want.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.350
I think it's called a paradox of choice

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:21.740
and it is an increasing problem

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:25.270
for a lot of people living
in the modern world.

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:28.500
And it happens when neither of the choices

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.370
are either good or bad

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:35.410
and you neither love
or hate either of them.

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:38.250
So your compass for making decision

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:39.920
just gets all screwed up.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.950
And when I look back at that time,

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:43.810
well, actually I still do that.

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:46.340
It was like a disease.

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:50.550
I was plagued by the little
decisions that I had to make,

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.650
and I didn't realize how much energy

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:55.520
it was taking out of my life

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:59.600
until I read this article by James Clear

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.340
on decision fatigue.

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:03.040
He talks about this research

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:06.320
by the National Academy of Sciences,

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.500
where they study 1,100 judicial hearings

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:11.860
over a 10-month period.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:14.500
So these were all parole board judges

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.090
who basically had to decide

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:19.280
if the criminal had to stay in jail

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.040
or if they could be released on parole.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:23.630
And what they realized

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:26.390
is that it wasn't actually the context

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:28.600
or the particulars of the crime

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.480
that had the biggest
impact on their decisions,

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:34.020
but it was actually the time of day.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:35.690
So at the beginning of the day,

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:40.203
the percentage of a favorable
ruling would be up about 65%.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:45.030
And near the end of the day,
it would drop down to zero.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:47.300
And this happened all across the board.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:49.320
And so after reading this article,

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.463
I decided to do a bit of
an extreme experiment.

00:01:55.001 --> 00:01:56.570
And I basically decided

00:01:56.570 --> 00:02:01.570
I'm gonna stop making any
small decisions. (laughs)

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:03.430
So if I went to a restaurant,

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:06.030
I would just ask the
staff recommendations.

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:07.760
And if I got ice cream,

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:10.600
I would just tell my friend to
get me whatever she's having.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.410
And then the second thing I started doing

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:15.020
is for any big decisions in my life,

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.850
I gave myself a time limit
to make those decisions

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:20.370
'cause I felt like, yeah,

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:22.940
it's actually a huge waste of my time

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:24.290
and I'm wasting my energy

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:27.740
to keep thinking about the same
problem over and over again.

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:30.100
So I actually did this for six months.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:33.270
I ate whatever someone else
ordered for me for six months.

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:38.270
But what I realized is that
I actually felt a visible,

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:41.980
like a weight lifted off of me.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:44.030
And I just had more energy

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.300
to make other decisions in my life.

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:50.800
So basically willpower is
like, it's like a muscle.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:53.310
If you keep using it throughout the day,

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:54.730
it's gonna get tired

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:57.410
and it's gonna fade
away throughout the day.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:00.230
So here's some slightly
more practical ways

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:04.950
you can reduce decision
fatigue and increase willpower.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:09.620
So the first one take a couple
of minutes the night before

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:12.140
to make all the small decisions.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:15.010
So plan out what you're gonna wear,

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:17.230
plan out what you're
gonna eat for breakfast.

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:20.280
This way you're not wasting
your precious willpower

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.220
on those little decisions.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:25.920
And number two, do the most
important things first.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:28.640
Studies have shown over
and over and over again

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:31.770
that your mind is more focused
and clear in the morning.

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:35.390
So whether it's learning a new language

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:36.930
or writing a new book,

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:38.280
try to do that right in the morning.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.710
And number three, schedule your tasks.

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:44.363
If you're, let's say
trying to lose weight,

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:48.090
schedule the workouts
right in your calendar.

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:52.027
If you trust yourself
to make that decision

00:03:52.027 --> 00:03:56.560
after a long day of work,
you are mistaken my friends.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:58.800
Your willpower is gonna run out

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:00.920
and it's gonna be harder
and harder for you

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:02.710
to make those hard decisions.

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:05.700
I schedule my writing right in the morning

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.600
from 6:00 a.m to 7:30 a.m.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:11.210
I actually started doing this not long ago

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:13.300
and I love it because I know

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:17.980
that once I get back from work, I'm tired.

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:21.070
All I'm gonna wanna do is
just chill on the couch.

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:23.200
And so, yeah, having that
right in my schedule,

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.850
it's a great way to keep consistent.

00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:30.850
And then number four is
eat, workout and recharge.

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:35.130
So the study that I was
talking about before

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:40.110
showed that the judges
favorable rulings spiked up

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:42.280
right after they had their lunch breaks.

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:45.790
Obviously if you're
eating pizza or junk food

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:49.040
and you go into a food coma after,

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:51.550
that's not really gonna work out.

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:53.470
So try to eat healthy foods

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:56.720
and also sneaking in a little
bit of exercise during the day

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:00.710
can also be a really
great way to recharge.

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:05.710
For me I bike to work
or I speed walk to work.

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:10.210
And that actually helps
me get really energized

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:11.610
for work in the morning.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:14.940
And if you wanna try going
the extreme route that I did

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:17.440
and try cutting out
all the small decisions

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:20.870
in your life, I would try it.

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:21.890
You'd be surprised

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:24.540
at how many of them really
doesn't make a difference.

